				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/buttfranklin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2721810); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2721810&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607028325" alt="Buttfranklin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2721810)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/buttfranklin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2721810); return false;" >Buttfranklin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1484049333 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 Jan 2017 11:55</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2723894">
									

<p>If anomalies exist, someone is going to use them to make money.</p>
<p>Faust made a deal with the devil and paid the price. What if you could make a million deals with a million devils — and get away scot-free?</p>
<p>Thanks to AidenEldritch, Shio and 9Volt for giving this a look-over.</p>
<p>Images: I made the &quot;schematic representation&quot; image with the free, open-source program Gephi, visualising its demo data set of, if I recall correctly, a dependency graph for a Java program. I made the &quot;case study&quot; image with Microsoft Publisher.</p>
<p>Because I can’t bear the idea of people not picking up on my countless self-pleasing references:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;wahoo!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;wahoo!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>A watchdog body that’s a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garmr">LITERAL watchdog of Hell</a>, lmao.</p>
<p>Images like <a href="http://www.finfacts.ie/irishfinancenews/article_1026675.shtml">these</a> were the inspiration for the case study in this article.</p>
<p>SPV usually stands for <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special-purpose_entity">something else</a>.</p>
<p>K2 can stand for <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K2_(tax_scheme)">this thing</a>.</p>
<p>“STC” is pretty close to <a href="http://blog.oup.com/2014/01/super-collider-ssc-academia-wall-street-quantitative-finance/">“SSC”</a>, an irl project that was meant to discover physical truths about reality, never got completed, and whose would-be researchers ended up working in the finance industry.</p>
<p>The “BBB‒” rating was meant to be an analogy to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bond_credit_rating">this sort of rating</a>.</p>
<p>Disclaimer: this is very much intended to be a heavy-handed, transparent metaphor for the global finance industry. The Foundation being utterly cowed by this bank is a direct metaphor for real-world regulatory bodies being similarly dominated by the institutions they’re supposed to police. Dell’Avaro joining ITCD from the Foundation is meant to be a direct metaphor for the “revolving door” phenomenon between policer and policed we see in the real world. The “oath subgroups” are meant to be a direct metaphor for real-world tax avoidance schemes and other ridiculously complicated systems set up by financial institutions. The whole of Bielefeld getting consumed is meant to be a direct metaphor for how ordinary people often bear the immense costs of mistakes made by financial institutions. Et cetera, et cetera, et cetera. Oh, and the &quot;distinctive extradimensional reality bender&quot; summoned with rituals are meant to be literal demons.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

