			<div class="content" id="post-content-2727338">
									

<p>The strikethroughs represent revisions; it's a common style thing here on the site, but it's also based on a real-world practice for various types of official documentation:</p>
<p>It started at the time when a correction was made by striking something out in a physical document and inserting any corrections between the lines with a ^ mark, but it's been retained in the typerwriter/digital era because gives both an in-document record of changes and can provide context/further clarification of the change being made. For example &quot;<span style="text-decoration: line-through;">widespread</span>&quot; emphasizes the fact they are now shooting for &quot;<em>zero</em>&quot;. You can see it frequently in things like national constitutions indicating parts changed or nullified by amendments, and some bank or inventory records indicating both the correction to an error and what that error originally was; both of which can potentially be useful.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>as having two edits of the same thing recorded implies that there's a version after the 2010 version but before the 2015 revision</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes. That's exactly what it means.</p>

